 1062 #uuid "5c3fc6b3-0d2e-50bb-98ce-b5e82b6f6a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065129 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1061 187691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7584e271-65ff-5831-8847-5f5e3a737754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{′}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1065 9132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1f70bcd-9b56-5fdd-ba18-58b625c05af0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1064 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1065 9136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e542fea-8d0a-5c0c-ba69-3ec1685ecf92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Derivatives Using Tables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234376 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2576954f-b0ee-571c-85ae-5533e6d22543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234376 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{'}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234379 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10dcb0f4-5f0f-5b93-a598-6c0cd2ed4435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234379 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{'}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01506915-9279-5845-be61-c4c35f7caa00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13eba5b3-5ee1-54a2-a165-599feede7427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e2f0aad-80c7-52df-b8b1-92ab89cc1a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74346772-ee82-5598-92fc-961da1e54b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "933b4cc2-b259-5d0b-aed0-2acad610e648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186244578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "35c107e1-441a-5e9e-a64c-c9d6f455a387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315375 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2be8b47e-8161-5ac6-af15-f112a84dd1b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317033 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317033 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317033 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317033 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317033 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cdde61d2-0b02-5fd6-a68f-661c818e9a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53ed59d8-b22e-538d-9cc1-0f9210effe07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317292 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1045 "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186409839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409822 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f566dc84-1e6c-5c3c-9fcc-e05918f8418d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409823 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409823 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409823 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ecb3b6c-5f9d-519e-ac19-a3e22b342ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409824 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409824 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f3f95aa-4ff5-5778-912a-4e72354aee90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409825 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409825 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409825 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58770571-0d16-5811-81f1-369401a6b261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409826 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409826 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409826 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59d1a2f1-8dc8-5d29-a302-08e96496fd6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409827 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17